=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that PC4 (VLAN 10, 172.16.10.4) cannot reach Laptop0 (VLAN 20, 172.16.20.10) — does not correspond to any real fault in the current lab state. Every component of the inter-VLAN routing path was inspected and found to be correctly configured and fully operational.

On **switch1**, PC4's access port (eth1) is bridged into `br_vlan10` and Laptop0's access port (eth7) is bridged into `br_vlan20`. The trunk uplink (eth0) carries 802.1Q-tagged sub-interfaces eth0.10 (VID 10) and eth0.20 (VID 20) into both respective bridges — correct trunk behavior. On **mswitch1**, the trunk toward switch1 (eth0) and the trunk toward router0 (eth4) both have matching VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 sub-interfaces bridged into `br_vlan10` and `br_vlan20` respectively — the inter-switch VLAN bridge is intact. On **router0**, sub-interfaces `eth0.10` (VID 10, 172.16.10.1/24) and `eth0.20` (VID 20, 172.16.20.1/24) are both UP and have the correct gateway addresses matching each host's configured default route. Live ping tests confirmed full bidirectional reachability: PC4 → Laptop0 (0% loss, ~2 ms), Laptop0 → PC4 (0% loss), and both hosts reach their respective default gateways. The ticket's premise — that inter-VLAN routing is broken — is false. No corrective action is required; the network is healthy.
=== END ===